{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "concern",
      "claim": "saffron/cell/session.py matches .saffron/policy.yaml's elevate_on: saffron/cell/**, so effective_risk will put this task at elevated regardless of the spec declaring no risk: field (it defaults to standard in frontmatter). At elevated, DESIGN.md's gate table makes size blocking rather than advisory, and the task sorts into DESIGN.md's level-4 ranking. This is correct, automatic repo behavior, not a bug, and the spec's measured token counts stay far under the ceiling either way, so nothing actually blocks. Separately, DESIGN.md's own risk table (the \"Saffron breaks Saffron\" row) says a self-task editing saffron/ should declare risk: elevated explicitly and put saffron/ in forbidden, which this spec does not do (it relies on the automatic elevate_on match and forbids only specific saffron/ subpaths). Since essentially every spec in this backlog touches saffron/ the same way, this DESIGN.md row reads as an illustrative risk/mitigation entry rather than an enforced per-spec rule, and no gate was found that requires a blanket saffron/ forbid.",
      "criterion": null,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "concern",
      "claim": "The agent note \"Give the critic cell's export a character outside ASCII, such as é in an added line of _ANCHORING_DIFF\" is ambiguous about whether to mutate the shared module-level _ANCHORING_DIFF constant in tests/test_session.py (reused in roughly 78 other call sites, several asserting exact equality against it, e.g. assert critic[0][2] == _ANCHORING_DIFF, assert applies == [_ANCHORING_DIFF, grown], and others depending on specific lines sitting outside every hunk of it) or to build a locally-scoped derived diff for just this new test, following the file's own existing precedent _GROWN_PATCH = _ANCHORING_DIFF + \"# the rebuttal's own fix\\n\". Read the mutating way, the change risks altering hunk shape that dozens of unrelated, currently-passing tests depend on; read the appending way (consistent with the _GROWN_PATCH precedent and with keeping the hunk anchored at src/x.py line 1), it is safe. The spec does not say which, so a builder could plausibly pick the risky reading, though the file's own convention and the line-1 anchoring instruction resolve the ambiguity for a careful reader.",
      "criterion": 1,
      "file": "tests/test_session.py",
      "line": 5690,
      "fixes": "witness"
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "estimated_lines: 13 in the frontmatter is small next to the measured 50 (+593 test-path) changed tokens the Notes for the agent section reports from a prototype. estimated_lines is read only by the external host-side driver.py check tool and by judge_estimate's plan-time forecast (which prices the plan's own estimate, not the frontmatter field) — no cell gate reads spec.estimated_lines directly, matching DESIGN.md's own claim that no cell gate reads it. The two numbers measure different things (author's rough line guess vs. measured token diff) and should not be compared directly by a reader expecting them to agree.",
